00:02:21.080 --> 00:02:22.760
why would I ever want a data structure that has&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:22.760 --> 00:02:26.320
a restricted interface and&nbsp;
gives me incorrect results?

00:02:26.320 --> 00:02:30.040
like many probabilistic data structures&nbsp;
the answer is that by giving up a bit in&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:30.040 --> 00:02:33.840
accuracy and flexibility we can save&nbsp;
an enormous amount in performance

00:02:33.840 --> 00:02:36.600
the catch is that you need a use&nbsp;
case where mistakenly identifying&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:36.600 --> 00:02:40.120
an element as in the set when it's not is okay

00:02:40.120 --> 00:02:43.760
that often occurs when you have a fast way to&nbsp;
compute something that works for most but not&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:43.760 --> 00:02:48.360
all elements and a much more expensive way&nbsp;
to compute the same thing that always works

00:02:48.360 --> 00:02:51.000
for our website we can use a&nbsp;
bloom filter to tell us yes&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:51.000 --> 00:02:56.480
a link is possibly bad or definitely&nbsp;
no this link is not a known bad link

00:02:56.480 --> 00:02:59.040
if the bloom filter says a link is possibly bad we&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:59.040 --> 00:03:01.840
fall back to the paid service&nbsp;
to give us a reliable answer

00:03:01.840 --> 00:03:04.240
so it's fine if we get a false&nbsp;
positive in a small percentage&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:04.240 --> 00:03:09.600
of cases that's still a win compared to&nbsp;
sending everything to the paid service

00:03:09.600 --> 00:03:11.720
great so how does a bloom filter work?

00:03:11.720 --> 00:03:14.760
think about identifying objects by their shadows

00:03:14.760 --> 00:03:17.640
shadows contain much less data than the originals

00:03:17.640 --> 00:03:21.800
but given an object you can usually&nbsp;
tell whether it's part of a shadow

00:03:21.800 --> 00:03:24.000
yes the shadows could conspire to trick you into&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:24.000 --> 00:03:28.760
seeing something that isn't there but in&nbsp;
reasonable cases you can work around it

00:03:28.760 --> 00:03:31.760
a bloom filter consists of a&nbsp;
block of memory that's M bits&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:31.760 --> 00:03:34.720
long along with some number K of hash functions

00:03:34.720 --> 00:03:37.760
in order to add an item to the&nbsp;
set we hash it using each hash&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:37.760 --> 00:03:42.200
function and set the bit at the&nbsp;
corresponding memory location to 1

00:03:42.200 --> 00:03:46.600
this pattern of bits from the hash&nbsp;
functions is like the object's shadow

00:03:46.600 --> 00:03:50.240
then to query if an element is in the&nbsp;
set we look for its shadow by using each&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:50.240 --> 00:03:53.880
hash function and checking whether&nbsp;
all the corresponding bits are set

00:03:53.880 --> 00:03:58.280
if any bit is a 0 then this item&nbsp;
was definitely not added to the set

00:03:58.280 --> 00:04:02.880
if every bit's a 1 it's either because&nbsp;
the element is actually in the set or&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:02.880 --> 00:04:06.360
because some combination of other&nbsp;
elements happen to set those bits

00:04:06.360 --> 00:04:10.440
so we can at best say the&nbsp;
element is possibly in the set

00:04:10.440 --> 00:04:12.280
that's really all there is to the bloom filter

00:04:12.280 --> 00:04:15.360
it uses a fixed amount of memory&nbsp;
no matter how many elements you add

00:04:15.360 --> 00:04:18.560
and the time it takes to add or query&nbsp;
elements only depends on how many and&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:18.560 --> 00:04:23.040
how slow your hash functions are not&nbsp;
how many elements were added already

00:04:23.040 --> 00:04:26.040
its implementation is rather&nbsp;
simple we'll get to that later

00:04:26.040 --> 00:04:30.880
all the difficulty lies in analyzing how much&nbsp;
memory and how many hash functions to use

00:04:30.880 --> 00:04:35.200
if we use M bits of memory, K hash&nbsp;
functions, and added N items what's the&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:35.200 --> 00:04:40.080
chance of falsely identifying an element&nbsp;
as in the set when actually it's not?

00:04:40.080 --> 00:04:42.920
in real life it depends, measure it

00:04:42.920 --> 00:04:45.440
but we can get a good approximate&nbsp;
answer if we assume the hash&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:45.440 --> 00:04:49.920
functions select bits uniformly and&nbsp;
independently for distinct elements

00:04:49.920 --> 00:04:54.120
for a fixed bit the chance that one&nbsp;
hash of a single element will set&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:54.120 --> 00:05:00.000
that bit is 1/M so the chance that&nbsp;
the bit is not set is 1 minus that

00:05:00.000 --> 00:05:06.600
for K independent hash functions to all not&nbsp;
set that bit we raise that to the power K

00:05:06.600 --> 00:05:11.040
and repeating this for N total&nbsp;
elements we raise that to the power N

00:05:11.040 --> 00:05:15.680
this is the chance that this fixed&nbsp;
bit is not set after adding N elements

00:05:15.680 --> 00:05:20.800
therefore the chance this fixed bit is set&nbsp;
after adding N elements is 1 minus that

00:05:20.800 --> 00:05:25.520
and a false positive means that K fixed bits&nbsp;
have all been set so we raise that to the power K

00:05:25.520 --> 00:05:29.520
to finally arrive at our estimate&nbsp;
of the false positive rate

00:05:29.520 --> 00:05:35.200
you can start plugging in numbers if you want or&nbsp;
using calculus we can approximate this as follows

00:05:35.200 --> 00:05:38.160
multiply and divide by M

00:05:38.160 --> 00:05:42.960
and then use the limit definition of e

00:05:42.960 --> 00:05:45.320
and then rearrange

00:05:45.320 --> 00:05:48.360
notice the quantity m/n is&nbsp;
something meaningful to us

00:05:48.360 --> 00:05:51.360
that's how many bits of storage&nbsp;
we've allocated per element

00:05:51.360 --> 00:05:54.680
if we fix that quantity to say&nbsp;
1 byte per element and plot

00:05:54.680 --> 00:05:57.120
we see that we don't need a&nbsp;
huge number of hash functions

00:05:57.120 --> 00:06:01.120
in fact the error rate is minimized&nbsp;
using around 5 hash functions

00:06:01.120 --> 00:06:05.240
more generally, using calculus&nbsp;
take derivatives set to zero etc.,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:05.240 --> 00:06:07.920
you can show that we can achieve&nbsp;
an error rate as small as we like

00:06:07.920 --> 00:06:11.760
by choosing the number of hash functions&nbsp;
to be about log base 2 of the desired&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:11.760 --> 00:06:17.320
error rate and the number of bits per&nbsp;
element to be about 1.44 times that

00:06:17.320 --> 00:06:21.240
so if we're okay with around a 2% false&nbsp;
positive rate then we could use five or&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:21.240 --> 00:06:24.320
six hash functions and eight bits per element

00:06:24.320 --> 00:06:28.960
for 10 million elements that's just 10 megabytes&nbsp;
of data regardless of how large each element is

00:06:28.960 --> 00:06:32.920
because the filter doesn't&nbsp;
store the actual elements

00:06:32.920 --> 00:06:37.040
okay but where can I get K different&nbsp;
hash functions for my data?

00:06:37.040 --> 00:06:40.280
instead of inventing a bunch of hash&nbsp;
functions there are a few tricks you can do

00:06:40.280 --> 00:06:42.360
like using a hash function that takes a seed or&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:42.360 --> 00:06:45.600
appending k different suffixes&nbsp;
to your data before hashing

00:06:45.600 --> 00:06:50.040
but here's a trick that can get the job&nbsp;
done using just one good hash function

00:06:50.040 --> 00:06:53.440
if I'm using 10 megabytes of&nbsp;
memory that's 80 million bits

00:06:53.440 --> 00:06:56.960
so my hash functions need to&nbsp;
output numbers up to 80 million

00:06:56.960 --> 00:07:01.480
higher is fine we'll just mod by the length&nbsp;
but we need at least up to 80 million

00:07:01.480 --> 00:07:08.440
log base 2 of 80 million is 26.25 so my&nbsp;
hashes really only need to be 27 bits long

00:07:08.440 --> 00:07:15.520
how about I use a 256-bit hash and then just&nbsp;
split the first 240 bits into 5 chunks of 48 bits

00:07:15.520 --> 00:07:18.360
treat each chunk as a separate&nbsp;
hash function and now I've got&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:18.360 --> 00:07:22.680
five 48-bit hash functions that&nbsp;
