00:07:27.480 --> 00:07:29.320
but for our use case it does work

00:07:30.680 --> 00:07:31.800
now let's implement it!

00:07:31.800 --> 00:07:34.920
it's super simple this is the whole implementation

00:07:34.920 --> 00:07:38.280
we take in the memory and a&nbsp;
function that computes the hashes

00:07:38.280 --> 00:07:40.960
I chose to use a function that&nbsp;
outputs multiple hashes rather&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:40.960 --> 00:07:45.240
than to store a list of hash functions&nbsp;
because hashes may share computation

00:07:45.240 --> 00:07:48.480
like first computing the 256-bit&nbsp;
hash then splitting that into&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:48.480 --> 00:07:54.840
5 48-bit chunks there's no need to&nbsp;
recompute the 256-bit hash five times

00:07:54.840 --> 00:07:56.480
estimating the false positive rate is just&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:56.480 --> 00:07:59.320
plugging into the formula&nbsp;
we had earlier in the video

00:07:59.920 --> 00:08:05.200
adding an element means calculating each&nbsp;
hash and setting the corresponding bit to one

00:08:05.200 --> 00:08:07.920
and checking if an element is in&nbsp;
the set means calculating each&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:08:07.920 --> 00:08:15.880
hash and checking whether any of&nbsp;
the corresponding bits are not set

00:08:15.880 --> 00:08:17.400
here we see how to split the output of a&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:08:17.400 --> 00:08:22.240
hash function into smaller ones&nbsp;
