00:09:03.920 --> 00:09:09.280
we can construct an array of zeros like this&nbsp;
allocating a byte for every eight bits rounded up

00:09:09.280 --> 00:09:12.960
getting a bit is just calculating which&nbsp;
byte and which bit within the byte using&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:09:12.960 --> 00:09:19.880
divmod then grabbing that index&nbsp;
and masking out the bit we want

00:09:19.880 --> 00:09:21.160
setting a bit is similar

00:09:21.160 --> 00:09:24.240
this first expression we do a&nbsp;
bitwise "AND" to clear the bit

00:09:24.240 --> 00:09:29.120
then the second one we do a bitwise&nbsp;
"OR" to set the value we want

00:09:30.360 --> 00:09:32.960
putting it all together we can use it like this

00:09:32.960 --> 00:09:38.320
we define a hash function in this case we just&nbsp;
use SHA256 and convert the result to an int

00:09:38.320 --> 00:09:43.920
we split our long hash into five&nbsp;
hashes that use 6 bytes or 48 bits each

00:09:43.920 --> 00:09:48.840
then we create our storage as a bit array&nbsp;
initialized to zeros and create the Bloom filter

00:09:48.840 --> 00:09:53.160
I made a little timer utility here that&nbsp;
just times each operation, not important

00:09:53.160 --> 00:09:57.920
we create 10 million random length-16&nbsp;
strings and add them to the Bloom filter

00:09:57.920 --> 00:10:02.400
note that we're using a huge amount of memory here&nbsp;
